WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.824
A hunt hypothesis is a focused, testable statement about activity that may be present in the environment.

00:00:07.824 --> 00:00:15.225
It gives the hunt direction and defines what evidence would support, weaken, or disprove the idea.

00:00:15.225 --> 00:00:21.780
A vague goal such as finding anything suspicious produces unfocused searches and uncertain conclusions.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:30.026
Threat-informed hypotheses can begin with relevant intelligence, but they must be adapted to the actual architecture and assets.

00:00:30.026 --> 00:00:37.427
Incident lessons, known control weaknesses, vulnerabilities, change history, and operator concerns can also identify useful questions.

00:00:37.427 --> 00:00:44.405
A remote-access hypothesis might ask whether vendor sessions reached assets outside approved destinations or times.

00:00:44.405 --> 00:00:52.652
An engineering hypothesis might ask whether a privileged workstation communicated with a controller without a matching work order.

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:59.629
A pathway hypothesis might ask whether systems crossed a zone boundary through an undocumented route.

00:00:59.629 --> 00:01:07.030
Write the hypothesis with scope, time range, assets, expected behavior, data sources, exclusions, and required reviewers.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:09.779
Identify blind spots before searching.

00:01:09.779 --> 00:01:18.026
If the available telemetry cannot answer the question, the hunt should say so rather than forcing a conclusion.

00:01:18.026 --> 00:01:22.466
Look for disconfirming evidence as well as supporting evidence.

00:01:22.466 --> 00:01:27.753
Planned maintenance, failover, equipment replacement, and troubleshooting may explain unusual activity.

00:01:27.753 --> 00:01:33.039
Confirmation bias is especially risky when technical patterns have process meaning.

00:01:33.039 --> 00:01:40.440
A completed hunt states the result and confidence: supported, not supported, inconclusive, or unable to test.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:46.995
Even a disproved hypothesis can improve baselines, inventory, telemetry coverage, and future detection design.
